-- title:
--   Node structure of the end local ring: crossing model, tangent, centre
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime and $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$; $\ell$ is a prime with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. Further, $L$ is a field of characteristic zero, $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity, and `hι` asks that there be a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = e^{2\pi i/q}$. The subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ is, by `hH₁`, the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, with the kernel of reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$; thus $H_1$ consists of the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ and modulo $\ell$. The intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq L((t))$ is, by `hK`, the $L$-subfield generated by the image under coefficient extension $\mathbb{Q} \to L$ of the $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$.
--
--   The base ring $A$ is a Henselian discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ and algebraically closed residue field, with $q \in \mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hAq`) and $\zeta$ in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), and $K$ is an $A$-algebra in a way compatible with $L$. The element $j \in K$ is nonzero and, by `hj`, has Laurent expansion the image in $L((t))$ of the rational $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular $j$-function. The element $\varpi$ generates $\mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hϖ`), and $\varpi_t$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\cdot(\text{unit})$ (`hϖt`). Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A K j`, the $A$-subalgebra of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$, and let $X :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) be the two-chart integral model, the pushout gluing $\operatorname{Spec} C$ and the corresponding chart at $j^{-1}$, with structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and with `ιFin` the morphism from $X_{\mathrm{fin}} = \operatorname{Spec} C$.
--
--   The ideal $y \subseteq C$ is maximal and contains $\varpi$ (`hy`, `hϖy`), and the supersingularity hypothesis `hss` requires that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$ one has $\varphi(j) \in$ [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero point killed by $q$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` is the rigidity input: for every point $z$ of $X$ such that the germ $\varpi_z$ at $z$ of $\varpi$ (pulled back along `toBase`, as pinned by `hϖz`) lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and every point $y'$ of $X_{\mathrm{fin}}$ with `ιFin` image $z$ at which the same supersingularity condition `hss'` holds, there exist a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak{m}_W = (\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f,u,v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f \equiv$ [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18) $= X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk of $X$ at $z$ onto $S = W[[X_0,X_1]]/\big(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))\,v - f u\big)$, satisfying seven clauses, stated for the concrete witness below.
--
--   Those seven clauses, for the data $z$, $\varpi_z$, $y'$ (with `hy'y` : $y' = y$ as ideals of $C$) and the witness $W_1$, $\sigma_1$, $f_1$, $u_1$, $v_1$, $e_1$ fixed in the statement, constitute the hypothesis `hW₁`. With the abbreviations $\mathrm{STK}$ for the stalk at $z$, $\mathrm{CMP}$ for its $\mathfrak{m}$-adic completion, $\mathrm{toC}$ for the completion map, $S$ the above quotient for $W_1$, $\mathrm{mkS}$ the quotient map, and $\mathrm{germY}$ the composite of the canonical identification $\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} C) \cong C$ with the germ map at $z$ along `ιFin`, they read: (1) $e_1$ carries the germ of $a \in A$ to the class of the constant $\sigma_1 a$; (2) for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $\tau \in \operatorname{Aut}_L(K)$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — the requirement that $\tau$ act on ratios of integral $q$-expansions of weight-$k$ modular forms on $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ as the substitution by `conjElemN q γ⁻¹`, after any embedding $\iota$ of $L$ into $\mathbb{C}$ sending $\zeta$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$ — the subalgebra $C$ is $\tau$-stable; (3) if moreover $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$, then the restriction of such a $\tau$ to $C$ is the identity modulo $y'$; (4) for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, a level automorphism $\tau$ as above preserving $C$ and acting trivially modulo $y'$, there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W_1$ and a matrix $M \in M_2(W_1)$ with: $\theta$ intertwining, through $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$, the action of $\tau$ on $C$; $\theta$ fixing all constants; $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0, X_1$; $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$ and $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$; $c \equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$; and $c \not\equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (5) tangent separation: for integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and primes $P_1,P_2$ of $S$, each not containing both classes of $X_0$ and $X_1$, each containing the class of $\sigma_1\varpi$, and each containing a form $a_iX_0 + b_iX_1 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, if $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$ then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ to the stalk along $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC}$ are distinct; (6) for a prime $P$ of $S$ as above containing $X_0 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, an element $a \in C$ has $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(a))$ in the contraction of $P$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of $\mathfrak{m}_A$ in $L$; (7) the series [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18), obtained from the $j$-expansion by multiplying all exponents by $q$, lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0 \in A$ with $\mathrm{jqN} - a_0 \in y'$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ whose degree-$e_0$ leading form has the property that $\sum_{i=0}^{e_0} \operatorname{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit for all $a,b \in W_1$ not both in $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ with $a^qb - ab^q \in \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$, such that $e_1(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\mathrm{jqN} - a_0))) = \mathrm{mkS}(h)$.
--
--   The remaining data are as follows. The ideal $J \subseteq C$ is, by `hJ`, the infimum of those ideals arising as the pullback along the restriction to $C$ of a level automorphism $\tau$ attached to some $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ of the contraction along $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$ of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $\sigma_1\varpi_t$, $X_0$ and $X_1$. The $A$-subalgebra $B \subseteq K$ is, by `hB`, generated over $C$ by the elements $x \in K$ with $x\varpi_t \in J$. A valuation subring $W \subseteq K$ contains $B$ (`hBW`). The hypothesis `hR1` states that $C \le B$ and that every element of $K$ is a quotient of elements of $B$; `hR2` states that $A \to B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation and that $B/\varpi B$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; `hR3` (five clauses) states that $W \cap L$ is exactly the image of $A$, that $\mathfrak{m}_W$ is generated by $\varpi$, that $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, that for $b \in C$ one has $b \in y$ if and only if $b \in \mathfrak{m}_W$, and that $W$ is the set of quotients $g/h$ with $g,h \in B$ and $h \notin \mathfrak{m}_W$. The hypothesis `hEQ` (five clauses, summarised here) requires: for every $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $k$ of $A$ a surjection $\rho : B \to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q k`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) with kernel the centre of $W$, compatible with $A \to k$, and equivariant for level automorphisms attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W$ through an element $c$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,\gamma, c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) acting by `hAction`, with $c \ne 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$; stability of $B$ under such $\tau$; that every prime of $B$ containing $\varpi$ contains the image under some such $\tau$ (with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$) of the centre of $W$; that an element of $B$ carried into $\mathfrak{m}_W$ by all such $\tau$ is divisible by $\varpi$; and that a $\tau$ preserving $y$ preserves $W$. The hypotheses `hjK`, `hjC` put [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) in $K$ and in $C$. Finally $m \ge 1$ satisfies $\varpi^m = \varpi_t\cdot(\text{unit})$ (`hmt`), and $O \subseteq K$ is a subring for which `hO` provides a nonzero $a \in J$ and a maximal ideal $P$ of the $A$-algebra $B_a$ generated over $C$ by the $x$ with $xa \in J$, such that $O$ is the set of quotients $g/h$ with $g,h \in B_a$, $h \notin P$, every element of $y$ is a non-unit in $O$, and $B \not\subseteq O$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses (with the abbreviations $\mathrm{STK}$, $\mathrm{CMP}$, $\mathrm{toC}$, $S$, $\mathrm{mkS}$, $\mathrm{germY}$ as above, for the witness $W_1$), the conclusion asserts: $O$ is a local ring, $O$ is Noetherian, and $C \subseteq O$ (via a witness `hCO` that every $b \in C$ lies in $O$), together with the following.
--
--   First, for $b \in C$ the image of $b$ lies in $\mathfrak{m}_O$ if and only if $b \in y$. Second, every $f \in O$ is congruent to a constant: there is $a \in A$ whose image lies in $O$ with $f - a$ a non-unit of $O$.
--
--   Third, there is a ring homomorphism $\Lambda : S \to \widehat{O}$, the $\mathfrak{m}_O$-adic completion, such that $\Lambda(e_1(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(c))))$ is the image of $c$ in $\widehat O$ for every $c \in C$, and such that for every $N$ and every $s$ in the $N$-th power of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $\sigma_1\varpi$, $X_0$ and $X_1$, the element $\Lambda(s)$ lies in the $N$-th power of the ideal of $\widehat O$ generated by the image of $\mathfrak{m}_O$.
--
--   Fourth, there is a ring isomorphism $\iota_1$ from $\widehat O$ onto the crossing model `UVCrossingModel W₁ ((σ₁ ϖ)^m)` $= W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/\big(X_0X_1 - (\sigma_1\varpi)^m\big)$ such that $\iota_1(\Lambda(\mathrm{mkS}(C(w)))) =$ `UVCrossingModel.const` $w$ for all $w \in W_1$; for every $a \in A$ whose image lies in $O$, $\iota_1$ carries the image of $a$ in $\widehat O$ to the constant $\sigma_1 a$; and every element of $W_1$ is congruent modulo $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ to some $\sigma_1 a$ with $a \in A$.
--
--   Fifth, there are $p_0, p_1 \in W_1$ and elements $\alpha, \beta$ of the crossing model with $p_0 \notin \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ or $p_1 \notin \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$, with $p_0^qp_1 - p_0p_1^q \in \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$, with $\alpha - \mathrm{const}(p_0)$ and $\beta - \mathrm{const}(p_1)$ non-units, and with $\iota_1(\Lambda(\mathrm{mkS}(X_0))) = \mathrm{V}\cdot\alpha$ and $\iota_1(\Lambda(\mathrm{mkS}(X_1))) = \mathrm{V}\cdot\beta$, where $\mathrm{U} =$ `UVCrossingModel.U ((σ₁ ϖ)^m)` and $\mathrm{V} =$ `UVCrossingModel.V ((σ₁ ϖ)^m)` are the distinguished elements of the crossing model.
--
--   Sixth, there are $a \in C$ with $a \in J$ and $a \ne 0$ in $K$, an element $c \in O$, and units $\gamma_U, \gamma_V$ of the crossing model such that every $i \in J$ is of the form $a\,o$ with $o \in O$, such that $a c = \varpi_t$ in $K$, and such that $\iota_1$ carries the image of $a$ in $\widehat O$ to $\gamma_V\cdot\mathrm{V}$ and the image of $c$ to $\gamma_U\cdot\mathrm{U}$.
--
--   This is the end-local step of the analysis of the auxiliary level-one model at a supersingular point: it identifies the completion of the local ring $O$ produced by the end pin with a crossing (node) model $W_1[[U,V]]/(UV - (\sigma_1\varpi)^m)$, records that the tangent direction cut out at the node is $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational, and shows that the weighted centre $J$ becomes principal in $O$, generated by an element whose image is a unit multiple of $V$ while $\varpi_t$ factors as that generator times an element mapping to a unit multiple of $U$. It feeds the trichotomy of exceptional primes in the Igusa-type analysis and the companion statement on tangent coordinates in the same frame.
